import { DocsPage, Demo, Code, ApiReference } from "../../shell.rx"
import { TooltipHero } from "../../demos/tooltip/hero.rx"

export default component Page() {
  <DocsPage title="Tooltip" subtitle="A short label that appears when a button is hovered or focused.">
    <Demo source="tooltip/hero"><TooltipHero /></Demo>

    <h2 id="anatomy">Anatomy</h2>
    <p>Import the parts and put them together:</p>
    <Code file="tooltip/anatomy.rx.txt" />

    <h2 id="platform">What the browser does</h2>
    <p>
      The popup is an element with <code>popover="manual"</code>, shown with{" "}
      <code>{"showPopover({ source: trigger })"}</code>, which makes the trigger its anchor. The browser
      provides:
    </p>
    <ul>
      <li>The top layer: the popup is drawn above everything else, without a portal or a <code>z-index</code>.</li>
      <li>Placement against the trigger with CSS anchor positioning, flipped to the other side when it doesn't fit.</li>
    </ul>
    <p>
      Popovers that open on hover (<code>interestfor</code>, <code>popover="hint"</code>) aren't in every engine
      yet, and a manual popover gets no light dismiss, so the library adds the rest: opening after a delay on
      hover and at once on keyboard focus, letting the pointer cross from the trigger onto the popup, the delay
      group, closing on <kbd>Escape</kbd>, on a press outside and on a press of the trigger, the{" "}
      <code>tooltip</code> role and the trigger's <code>aria-describedby</code>, and <code>data-side</code> with
      the arrow's position.
    </p>
    <div class="note">
      <p>
        The trigger has no <code>popovertarget</code>: pressing it must not open a tooltip. The popup stays in the
        document while it is closed, so there is no Portal part, and it is the positioned element itself, so there
        is no Positioner.
      </p>
    </div>

    <h2 id="examples">Examples</h2>

    <h3 id="provider">Grouping tooltips</h3>
    <p>
      Without a provider every tooltip waits its delay. Inside a <code>TooltipProvider</code>, once one tooltip has
      opened, moving to another trigger opens its tooltip at once and closes the first. That lasts until{" "}
      <code>timeout</code> milliseconds after the last one closed (400 by default). The provider's{" "}
      <code>delay</code> and <code>closeDelay</code> apply to every trigger beneath it that doesn't set its own.
      The demo above wraps its three buttons in one provider.
    </p>

    <h3 id="delays">Delays and closing</h3>
    <p>
      A trigger opens its tooltip once the pointer has rested on it for <code>delay</code> milliseconds (600 by
      default) and closes it <code>closeDelay</code> milliseconds after the pointer leaves (0 by default). Keyboard
      focus opens it without waiting, and moving focus away closes it. Pressing the trigger closes the tooltip and
      it doesn't come back until the pointer has left; pass <code>{"closeOnClick={false}"}</code> to keep it
      open.
    </p>
    <p>
      The pointer may move from the trigger onto the popup, crossing the gap between them, and the tooltip stays
      open while it is there. With <code>disableHoverablePopup</code> on the root it closes as soon as the pointer
      leaves the trigger.
    </p>

    <h3 id="controlled">Controlling it</h3>
    <p>
      Pass <code>open</code> and <code>onOpenChange</code> to the root to hold the state yourself. Hover, focus,{" "}
      <kbd>Escape</kbd> and presses don't change a controlled tooltip: they call <code>onOpenChange</code> with the
      state they ask for. A <code>disabled</code> root never opens, and closes if it was open.
    </p>

    <h3 id="placement">Placement and the arrow</h3>
    <p>
      The popup sits above its trigger, centred, unless <code>side</code> and <code>align</code> say otherwise;{" "}
      <code>sideOffset</code> is the gap. These become CSS anchor positioning declarations in the popup's inline
      style, which a stylesheet can replace. When the browser flips the popup, its <code>data-side</code> changes
      to the side it ended up on. <code>TooltipArrow</code> is placed on the edge that faces the trigger, centred
      on the trigger; give it a shape and turn it per <code>data-side</code>, as the demo does.
    </p>

    <h3 id="animation">Animating it</h3>
    <p>
      Animate the popup in with <code>@starting-style</code> under <code>:popover-open</code>. The library hides the
      popover as soon as the tooltip closes, so animating it out is left to CSS: transition <code>display</code>{" "}
      and <code>overlay</code> with <code>allow-discrete</code>, which takes effect in the engines that support it
      and is ignored in the others.
    </p>

    <h2 id="api-reference">API reference</h2>
    <ApiReference family="tooltip" />
  </DocsPage>
}
